            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus views butter (curds) primarily as an emblem of material abundance, prosperity, and divine provision, though its meaning is highly context-dependent, sometimes representing scarcity, deceptive flattery, or hospitality. There is no settled, uniform symbolic meaning.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines butter allegorically as 'the word churned solid' or 'sound doctrine' produced by labor, which provides moral discernment and spiritual nourishment.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Material abundance, prosperity, or a context-dependent natural metaphor (e.g., simple nourishment, smooth speech).\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Solidified sound doctrine produced by laboring over the Word.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus restricts butter to a figure of natural prosperity, provision, or specific contextual metaphors, while the book allegorizes it into a specific spiritual substance (sound doctrine) derived from milk (the Word).\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus identifies butter in passages like Isaiah 7 and Job 29 as natural food representing either luxury/abundance or pastoral survival; the book asserts it symbolizes mature spiritual doctrine that imparts moral discernment.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book introduces a systematic allegorical definition ('the word churned solid') that replaces the consensus's primary identification of material prosperity and pastoral provision. While it incorporates the contextual themes (remnant's food, counterfeit speech), its core definition fundamentally diverges from the established scholarly baseline.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The claim that butter symbolizes 'the word churned solid' relies on an external allegorical premise (milk = the Word; churning =